<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achimota School</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince of Wales College</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achimota School is a boarding school near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, Ghana, founded in the 1920s by Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg">Gordon Guggisberg</a>, the Reverend Alexander Fraser and James Kwegyir Aggrey and opened in 1927 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> College. Co-educational from the start, it trained much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>'s later elite, and its former pupils include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> and several later heads of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achimota-school.jpg" alt="Achimota School" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kandymotownie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACHIMOTA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> saw the need for advanced education. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg">Frederick Gordon Guggisberg</a>, the governor, argued that the gap between the African who had completed his education at an English University and the semi-educated African of the primary school was dangerously wide. After the Phelps-Stokes Commission's report of 1920 he appointed a committee in 1922, which recommended a comprehensive institution at Achimota for secondary education, teacher training and technical education. Chiefs such as Nana Sir Ofori Atta and Nene Sir Emmanuel Mate Kole supported the plan.</p><p>The Legislative Council approved the 1923–24 budget for the Prince of Wales College and School, and Guggisberg laid the foundation stone in March 1924. Alexander G. Fraser, formerly principal of Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, was the first principal (1924–1935) and James Aggrey the first vice-principal (1924–1927). Aggrey held that educating a woman benefited a whole family and community, and the college increased its places for girls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Guggisberg formally opened the school on 28 January 1927, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, as guest of honour. Modelled on the British public school, Achimota was the first mixed school on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. Its motto, Ut Omnes Unum Sint, That they all may be one, expressed the founders' aim that black and white, male and female should combine for the good of all. Of the piano-key crest Aggrey said that for perfect harmony &quot;you must use both the black and the white keys&quot;.</p><p>The University College of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, now the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology grew out of Achimota College and its Engineering School. Its pupils and teachers included Kwame Nkrumah, Edward Akufo-Addo, Jerry John Rawlings and John Evans Atta Mills, later heads of state of Ghana, as well as Kofi Abrefa Busia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The school occupies 525 hectares in the middle of the Achimota Forest Reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. Its pupils live in seventeen houses named after founders, missionaries and alumni; Gyamfi House recalls a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> royal family who died while a student, after which the Asante Kingdom founded Prempeh College. From the start Achimota stressed agriculture and practical skills, and its music led to the establishment of the Ghanaian National Symphony Orchestra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Achimota School” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
